\n  - A temporary pause is not the permanent ban in rung 1, \"Prohibit artificial intelligence tools in student work and classroom instruction\".\n  - He says nothing about teachers using AI for planning, so it can't tell rung 1 apart from rung 2.\n- **\"Get Big Tech out of the classroom\" policy page.** It also argues against rung 1. It ties the pause to a review that will set \"the appropriate grade level for AI to be introduced into the classroom\", which expects AI in classrooms later. I coded the page `third-party-characterization`, because the codebook treats a campaign page written in the third person (\"STEVE'S PLAN WILL\") as someone describing the person. I gave it the `study-directive` shape.\n- **Smartphone page.** It mentions AI only in passing and its policy is a phone ban. I coded it `adjacent` and `rhetorical`.\n\n**Quotes:** I tiered both pause quotes `lever` and quotable. The policy-page quote also carries `lever-named`, because it names the review.\n\n**`needs_source`** is empty: the row rests on campaign words, and no named record (bill or vote) is missing from the sources.\n\n**One judgement for review:** coding the third-person policy page as characterization follows the V1 rule as written. Read the other way, it would be `statement-answer` own words, and it would still give `direction-only`.","ttft_ms":39731,"type":"result","duration_ms":64018,"uuid":"1113904b-e17d-4764-9082-f0b1f14589e9","ttft_stream_ms":18720,"time_to_request_ms":146,"first_content_frame_ms":18720,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
